# infix: a calculator with precedence, parentheses and signs. One expression per line in, its value on a line out.
#
#   4 + 3 * 2        writes 10       * and / bind tighter than + and -
#   (4 + 3) * 2      writes 14       parentheses, as deep as you like
#   2 - 3 - 4        writes -5       the same precedence goes left to right
#   2 * -3           writes -6       a + or - where a number should start is a sign
#   4 - -3           writes 7        and so is a second one: --3 writes 3, 2 - - 3 writes 5
#   -(4 + 3) * 2     writes -14      a sign in front of a ( too
#   7 / 2            writes 3        division rounds down; dividing by 0 leaves the left side as it was
#
# Whole numbers, + - * / and ( ), with spaces anywhere between them. A sign belongs to the whole term it
# stands in, so 7 / -2 writes -3, like -7 / 2. The last line needs no newline, an empty line writes
# nothing, and both stacks are empty when the input ends. Numbers are 64-bit and wrap.
#
# The idea. No stack of operators and no stack of values: for every group that is open, the line itself
# and each ( inside it, the main stack holds a frame of four numbers and a flag on top of a slot S for
# the group's sum:
#
#   S | sgn T M N F    S    the sum of the terms finished so far
#                      sgn  1 or -1: the term being built is added or subtracted
#                      T    the product so far within the term
#                      M    what the next number does to T: 0 multiplies, positive divides
#                      N    the number being read
#                      F    1 once a digit or a ) has come since the last operator, 0 before
#
# A new frame is 1 1 0 0 0: plus one times the number to come, which has not begun. A digit d makes
# N = 10N + d and F = 1. Every operator, ) and the end of a line first looks at F: a + or - while F is 0
# is a sign, which multiplies sgn by 1 or -1. Anything else does T = T*N or T/N, and then * and / set
# M N; + and - add sgn*T to S and start a new term; ( pushes a frame; ) adds sgn*T to S, and S lies where
# the outer N was, which is where the group's value belongs; the end of a line does the same and writes
# S. The stash keeps a 10 at its bottom for the whole line: the digit trail copies it for 10N, the
# operator trail for c - 42, and the writing ring writes it last, as the newline.
#
#   ring: empty the stack --> L: read c ----- the end of input: halt
#     ^      ^                 |    '-------- a space, a newline: round again
#     |      '-----------------'
#     |                        v
#     |             the first character: a 10 into the stash, a frame under c
#     |                        v
#     |    M: read c --> c divmod 16 ---- a digit: N = 10N + r, F = 1 -----------------> M
#     |                    |     |
#     |      the line ends |     '-- x = c - 42 ---- a space --------------------------> M
#     |                    |              |   '----- ( : a frame out of the -2 --------> M
#     |                    v              v
#     |              F + q*q <-------- x divmod 5:  ) -1 4   * 0 0   + 0 1   - 0 3   / 1 0
#     |                    |--- 0: a sign, sgn = sgn * (2 - r) -------------------------> M
#     |                    v
#     |              T = T*N or T/N
#     |                    |--- * / : the two numbers are the new M N -----------------> M
#     |                    v
#     |              S = S + sgn*T
#     |                    |--- ) : S is the outer N ----------------------------------> M
#     |                    |--- + - : a new term ---------------------------------------> M
#     |                    v
#     '--- write the stash <-- the digits into the stash <-- the sign
#
# A character is sorted by three pinks, three ways each: on 2 - c/16 (digit, line end, the rest), on
# c - 40 (space, the opening parenthesis, an operator) and, after the arithmetic, on the quotient of
# (c - 42)/5; one more, on F + q*q, tells a sign from an operator. The end of a line travels the operator
# trail as an operator of its own, so the arithmetic is there once. Seven trails come back to the reading
# black. Six of them meet in a funnel, orange brown orange brown, which does nothing: a trail that has
# one number too many joins at a brown, the others at an orange, and the signs and * / meet at one more
# orange before it, which copies their 0 as the flag. No strip has more than two ways in. Three counted
# rings do the rest: one empties the main stack when a line starts, one cuts the digits off into the
# stash (it goes round to the right: the pink tests q - 2q), one writes the stash. The trails never cross.
#
# Malformed input never hangs and leaves nothing behind for the next line. A missing number counts as 0
# (3* writes 0, () writes 0); spaces inside a number are not there; a ( that is never closed makes its
# group the answer; a ) too many gives nonsense; 5(2) writes 8 and (2)3 writes 23; a tab, a carriage
# return or any other control character ends the line, so CRLF lines work; ! " # $ % & ' are skipped like
# spaces; after a number a comma drops the next term and a full stop subtracts it twice, and where a
# number should start * , . are signs worth 2, 0 and -2 (2+*3 writes 8); : ; < = > ? and letters are
# digits worth their code modulo 16. -9223372036854775808 is written as -2.
#
# Not supported: powers, decimals, and messages about mistakes.
#
# Made by gen_infix.py from a layout search; do not edit by hand. Every strip that must not link is more
# than 1600 from every other, and every turn is decided by more than 27 degrees.
#
# The start of a line: empty the main stack, then read until something that is not a space.
